  @3234256: [ego-browser:skill] ego-browser usage guide v2.1.0
  @3234308: # ego-browser
  @3234323: Call `help()` or `help("page.click")` to print signatures and uncommon options
  @3234402: of APIs named below; it prints by itself, so do not wrap it in `console.log`.
  @3234480: For installation or connection problems, read `references/install.md` in the
  @3234557: ego-browser Skill directory.
  @3234587: ## Run browser scripts
  @3234611: Pass the script to `ego-browser nodejs`. The form depends on the shell that runs
  @3234692: the command: heredoc is Bash/Zsh syntax and does not exist in PowerShell or cmd.
  @3234774: ### Bash, Zsh, Git Bash
  @3234799: ```bash
  @3234807: ego-browser nodejs <<'EOF'
  @3234834: const task = await taskSpace("inspect example page");
  @3234888: const page = task.page("p1");
  @3234918: await page.goto("https://example.com");
  @3234959: console.log({ taskSpaceId: task.spaceId, page: page.label });
  @3235021: console.log(await page.snapshot());
  @3235066: When a sandbox blocks heredoc input, use `-e` instead. Use single quotes around
  @3235146: the code and double quotes for JavaScript strings; single quotes within the code
  @3235227: require shell quoting.
  @3235251: ```bash
  @3235259: ego-browser nodejs -e '
  @3235283: const task = await taskSpace("inspect example page");
  @3235337: await task.page("p1").goto("https://example.com");
  @3235388: console.log(task.spaceId);
  @3235422: ### PowerShell
  @3235438: PowerShell has no heredoc, and Windows PowerShell 5.1 drops the double quotes
  @3235516: inside an argument when it calls a native program, which splits the script into
  @3235596: several arguments. Do not pass a script that contains double quotes or spans
  @3235673: several lines to `-e`. Write it to a UTF-8 `.mjs` file instead and evaluate an
  @3235752: ASCII-only one-line entry point, which needs no double quotes of its own:
  @3235827: ```powershell
  @3235841: ego-browser nodejs -e "await import('file:///C:/Users/<name>/ego/task.mjs')"
  @3235923: The imported module runs with the same helpers and `console` as an inline
  @3235997: script, so the whole task belongs in the `.mjs` file. Use forward slashes in the
  @3236078: `file:///` URL. Non-ASCII content such as Chinese text must stay inside the
  @3236154: file: never put it in a `-e` argument.
  @3236194: Do not pipe the file in with `Get-Content`. Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a UTF-8
  @3236275: file as ANSI and sends ASCII to native programs, which corrupts non-ASCII text
  @3236354: and truncates strings. PowerShell has no `<` input redirection either.
  @3236426: ### cmd
  @3236435: cmd has no heredoc, but it does support input redirection, so it can run the
  @3236512: same `.mjs` file directly:
  @3236540: ```bat
  @3236547: ego-browser nodejs < C:\Users\<name>\ego\task.mjs
  @3236602: ### Later rounds with a script file
  @3236639: Each run is a new process, and earlier rounds have already run. Write a new,
  @3236716: complete file for every round (`round-01.mjs`, `round-02.mjs`, ...) instead of
  @3236795: editing the previous one, which would replay old steps. Keep one goal's files in
  @3236876: a new directory named for the goal plus the date and time, such as
  @3236943: `C:/Users/<name>/ego/laptop-prices-20260922-1405/`, and only run files written
  @3237022: in this conversation. Create files with your file tool, not PowerShell
  @3237093: `Set-Content`, `Out-File`, or `>`, which write ANSI or UTF-16 on 5.1.
  @3237164: ### When the script fails to parse
  @3237200: `SyntaxError: await is only valid in async functions and the top level bodies of
  @3237281: modules` almost always means the shell damaged the script before Node received
  @3237360: it; top-level `await` itself is supported. Do not wrap the code in an async
  @3237436: IIFE, open a new task space, or resend the same command. Switch to the `.mjs`
  @3237514: file form above.
  @3237532: The script always runs in Node.js, not in the web Page. Browser helpers and
  @3237608: Node.js APIs belong in the script; Page globals such as `window`, `document`,
  @3237686: `location`, and DOM APIs do not. Put browser-side JavaScript inside
  @3237754: `page.evaluate()`. Do not import Playwright or launch another browser.
  @3237826: The Node.js runtime uses ESM. When a script needs local files, load built-ins
  @3237904: with dynamic imports such as `await import("node:fs/promises")`. The script
  @3237980: does not run in your shell's working directory, so use absolute paths for every
  @3238060: file it reads or writes. It also does not inherit your shell's environment
  @3238135: variables: `process.env` will not contain values you set before running
  @3238207: `ego-browser`, so write every input directly into the script.
  @3238270: Ego-browser deliberately exposes a small custom API. It is not Playwright, even
  @3238350: where method names and options look similar. Use only the TaskSpace, Page,
  @3238425: FileChooser, mouse, and keyboard APIs explicitly listed in this Skill. Do not
  @3238503: infer Playwright methods such as `locator()`, `getByRole()`, `context()`,
  @3238577: `expect()`, or `route()`. When the listed API does not cover an operation, use
  @3238656: the documented `page.evaluate()` or `page.cdp()` escape hatches instead of
  @3238731: guessing another method.
  @3238757: Only these pointer actions accept an optional `label` option: `click()`,
  @3238830: `dblclick()`, `hover()`, `dragAndDrop()`, `mouse.click()`, `mouse.move()`, and
  @3238909: `mouse.wheel()`. Pass a concise 3-6 word description to keep the action text
  @3238986: next to the visible agent cursor in sync with the action. Other methods, such
  @3239064: as `fill()`, `press()`, `focus()`, `selectOption()`, `waitForSelector()`,
  @3239138: `keyboard.*()`, `mouse.down()`, and `mouse.up()`, reject a `label` option.
  @3239213: The `label` in `selectOption(selector, { label })` is different: it selects the
  @3239293: option whose visible text matches.
  @3239329: When the user explicitly asks for ego-browser, start with a real browser command
  @3239410: and diagnose the CLI or installation only if it fails.
  @3239466: ## Spaces, rounds, and pages
  @3239496: - Use exactly one TaskSpace for the entire user goal. Create it once, print its
  @3239576:   `spaceId`, and resume that same space in later rounds. Use multiple spaces
  @3239653:   only when the user explicitly requests them.
  @3239700: - Task space names are unique. `taskSpace(name)` resumes your own space with
  @3239777:   that name instead of creating a second one. If the name belongs to a
  @3239848:   user-owned space, it fails with `EGO_TASK_SPACE_NAME_IN_USE`: ask the user
  @3239925:   whether to work in that space (then `claimTaskSpace(id)`), or pick another
  @3240002:   name.
  @3240010: - Never use a new TaskSpace to recover from a stuck, blocked, timed-out, or
  @3240086:   unexpected Page. Recover within the existing space; if it cannot continue,
  @3240163:   stop and ask the user.
  @3240188: - Every invocation starts a new Node.js process. Task spaces, tabs, and Page labels
  @3240272:   persist; JavaScript variables do not.
  @3240312: - A new task space starts with Page `p1`; navigate it instead of opening
  @3240385:   another Page.
  @3240401: - Reuse a Page with `goto()` instead of opening a new Page for every URL.
  @3240475: - All time values are milliseconds.
  @3240512: ```js
  @3240518: // Later round: use the space id and Page label printed earlier.
  @3240583: const resumed = await taskSpace(7);
  @3240619: const source = resumed.page("p1");
  @3240654: await source.goto("https://example.com/releases");
  @3240710: Do not inspect or select profiles unless the user explicitly requests a
  @3240782: particular Ego Lite profile. A `profileId` applies only when creating a space;
  @3240861: use `help("profiles")` for the exact workflow.
  @3240909: Supported TaskSpace API:
  @3240935: - State: `spaceId`, `name`, `ownership`, `page(label)`, `userPage()`
  @3241004: - Pages: `await task.pages()`, `await task.tabs()`, `newPage()`,
  @3241069:   `adopt(page, { as? })`, `release(label)`
  @3241112: - Control: `waitForControl(options)`, `handOff()`, `finish({ keep })`
  @3241182: - Advanced: `cdp(method, params, options)`
  @3241226: Pages receive permanent labels such as `p1`, `p2`, and `p3`. Prefer these labels
  @3241307: to custom `{ as }` values. Reuse or close Pages as the task proceeds; the runtime
  @3241389: reports the configured Page budget when it is reached.
  @3241445: `task.newPage()` creates another blank Page when multiple Pages must stay open.
  @3241525: Navigate it separately with `page.goto()`.
  @3241569: `task.page(label)` only refers to a Page that already exists; it never creates
  @3241648: one, and its handle is always truthy, so `task.page("p2") || await task.newPage()`
  @3241731: and `if (!page)` do not detect a missing label. Use labels printed by earlier
  @3241809: output or by `await task.pages()`, and call `await task.newPage()` to create one.
  @3241892: `await task.pages()` returns managed Pages. `await task.tabs()` returns every tab in the
  @3241981: space as `{ label?, page, targetId, title, url, active, openedBy }`. A tab
  @3242056: without a label is unmanaged; adopt it before operating:
  @3242114: ```js
  @3242120: const active = (await task.tabs()).find((item) => item.active);
  @3242184: if (active && !active.label) {
  @3242215:   const page = await task.adopt(active.page);
  @3242261:   console.log({ page: page.label, url: await page.url() });
  @3242328: `release(label)` returns an unknown-origin Page to the user without closing its
  @3242408: tab. Close Agent-created Pages with `page.close()`. Treat `openedBy: "unknown"`
  @3242488: as user-owned when deciding whether a Page may be closed.
  @3242547: ## Page operations
  @3242567: ego-browser provides the following Page API:
  @3242613: - State and observation: `label`, `spaceId`, `openedBy`, `targetId`, `url()`,
  @3242691:   `title()`, `info()`, `snapshot()`, `screenshot()`
  @3242743: - Navigation and waits: `goto()`, `reload()`, `waitForURL()`,
  @3242805:   `waitForEvent()`, `waitForSelector()`, `waitForLoadState()`,
  @3242868:   `waitForFunction()`, `waitForTimeout()`
  @3242910: - Elements: `click()`, `dblclick()`, `hover()`, `dragAndDrop()`, `fill()`,
  @3242985:   `selectOption()`, `focus()`, `press()`, `setInputFiles()`,
  @3243046:   `waitForFileChooser()`, `close()`
  @3243082: - Dialogs: `acceptDialog(promptText?)`, `dismissDialog()`
  @3243140: - Pointer: `mouse.click()`, `move()`, `down()`, `up()`, `wheel()`
  @3243206: - Keyboard: `keyboard.down()`, `up()`, `press()`, `type()`, `insertText()`,
  @3243282:   `paste()`
  @3243294: - Page code and protocols: `evaluate(fnOrString, argument)`,
  @3243355:   `fetch(url, options)`, `cdp(method, params, options)`
  @3243412: `page.evaluate()` callbacks run only inside the Page; they cannot read variables
  @3243493: or Node.js modules from the surrounding script. Define browser-side helpers
  @3243569: inside the callback or pass one JSON-serializable value as its second argument.
  @3243650: Work efficiently:
  @3243669: - Each time you observe, collect only the cheapest page state sufficient to
  @3243745:   choose the next action. Use a snapshot for semantic or locator ground truth
  @3243823:   and a screenshot for visual confirmation; do not request both by default.
  @3243899: - If an action does not produce the expected result, inspect the current page
  @3243977:   before deciding whether to retry. Do not blindly repeat it or immediately
  @3244053:   fall back to coordinates or raw CDP.
  @3244092: - Once the page clearly shows the requested result, stop; do not confirm the
  @3244169:   same result through multiple surfaces.
  @3244211: ### Semantic pages: snapshot and selectors
  @3244255: Prefer snapshots and semantic selectors for ordinary DOM pages. Use screenshots
  @3244335: and coordinates only when useful DOM semantics are unavailable.
  @3244400: Before choosing an unfamiliar target, take a snapshot. When the current state
  @3244478: is sufficient to plan several actions on the same Page, complete them in one
  @3244555: script invocation, then observe the result once. Observe between actions only when an
  @3244641: intermediate result changes what should happen next. Keep the action sequence,
  @3244720: the wait for its final expected state, and the next snapshot in the same
  @3244793: script invocation. Print the snapshot last so the next round can act on it directly.
  @3244878: The final snapshot is the next round's starting view of the changed page;
  @3244952: without it, that round usually has to spend a separate browser call observing
  @3245030: before it can choose the next target, which wastes compute.
  @3245091: Wait for the expected result: use `waitForURL()` for navigation,
  @3245156: `waitForSelector()` for element state, or `waitForFunction()` for application
  @3245234: state. Avoid fixed delays when an observable condition exists. A snapshot
  @3245308: captures the current moment; it does not wait for the page to become stable.
  @3245385: `page.snapshot()` captures the current viewport. For content outside it, use
  @3245462: `page.snapshot({ scope: "full_page" })`.
  @3245504: The default viewport snapshot includes visible iframe content returned by the
  @3245582: browser. To focus on a frame's subtree, reuse the ref printed on its `iframe`
  @3245660: line:
  @3245667: ```js
  @3245673: console.log(await page.snapshot({ scope: "subtree", root: "@12" }));
  @3245747: Use the refs returned by the subtree for actions inside the iframe. A subtree
  @3245825: snapshot does not scope later locator actions; they still prefer actionable
  @3245901: matches in the top document before searching frames.
  @3245955: `waitForLoadState()` defaults to `load`. `waitForFunction()` follows the
  @3246028: Playwright argument order; pass `undefined` before options when there is no Page
  @3246109: argument:
